ACC-019 — Cognitive Lifecycle

Document ID: ACC-019
Title: Cognitive Lifecycle
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Cognitive Lifecycle of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Cognitive Lifecycle describes the progression of engineering cognition from the observation of Engineering Reality through the creation of Engineering Understanding, the preservation of Engineering Memory, the maturation of Engineering Knowledge, and the delivery of Decision Support.

It establishes the normative lifecycle governing engineering cognition while supporting continuous evolution, validation, refinement, and reuse.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all cognitive artifacts and processes defined by the Atlas Core Concepts.

The Cognitive Lifecycle governs:

Engineering Observation
Engineering Evidence
Engineering Understanding
Engineering Memory
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Reasoning
Engineering Capability
Decision Support

The lifecycle is iterative rather than strictly sequential.

3. Canonical Definition

The Cognitive Lifecycle is the continuous process through which engineering cognition is acquired, validated, understood, preserved, generalized, applied, evaluated, and refined over time.

Engineering cognition is not static.

It continuously evolves as Engineering Reality and Engineering Evidence change.

4. Lifecycle Phases
4.1 Observe

Engineering Reality is observed through measurements, inspections, monitoring, simulations, documentation, or other engineering activities.

Input

Engineering Reality

Output

Engineering Observation
4.2 Validate

Engineering Observations are evaluated for relevance, integrity, and applicability.

Input

Engineering Observation

Output

Engineering Evidence
4.3 Understand

Engineering Evidence is integrated into a coherent representation of Engineering Reality.

Input

Engineering Evidence

Output

Engineering Understanding
4.4 Preserve

Engineering Understanding is retained together with supporting context, evidence, and provenance.

Input

Engineering Understanding

Output

Engineering Memory
4.5 Generalize

Repeatedly validated Engineering Understanding evolves into reusable Engineering Knowledge.

Input

Engineering Memory

Output

Engineering Knowledge
4.6 Reason

Engineering Understanding and Engineering Knowledge are analyzed to derive engineering conclusions.

Inputs

Engineering Understanding
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Context
Engineering Constraints

Output

Engineering Conclusions
4.7 Execute

Engineering Capabilities apply Engineering Reasoning to accomplish engineering objectives.

Input

Engineering Conclusions

Output

Engineering Capability Results
4.8 Support

Engineering Capability results are transformed into actionable Decision Support.

Input

Engineering Capability Results

Output

Decision Support
4.9 Learn

Engineering outcomes, new observations, feedback, and validation continuously refine Engineering Understanding, Engineering Memory, and Engineering Knowledge.

This phase closes the cognitive loop.

