6. Cross-Cutting Concepts

Throughout the Cognitive Lifecycle, engineering cognition SHALL be governed by:

Engineering Context
Engineering Intent
Engineering Constraints

and qualified through:

Engineering Confidence
Engineering Traceability
Engineering Explainability

These concepts apply to every lifecycle phase.

7. Responsibilities

The Cognitive Lifecycle is responsible for:

Maintaining continuity of engineering cognition.
Supporting continuous learning.
Enabling engineering adaptation.
Preserving engineering traceability.
Supporting explainable engineering reasoning.
Facilitating reusable engineering knowledge.
Closing the engineering learning loop.
8. Normative Requirements

The Cognitive Lifecycle SHALL:

Preserve traceability between lifecycle phases.
Support continuous refinement.
Permit revision of Engineering Understanding when new evidence becomes available.
Preserve Engineering Memory during evolution.
Allow Engineering Knowledge to mature through repeated validation.
Support iterative engineering cognition rather than one-time execution.

Atlas SHALL NOT treat the Cognitive Lifecycle as a fixed linear workflow.

9. Non-Goals

The Cognitive Lifecycle does not:

Define implementation workflows.
Prescribe software architecture.
Replace engineering methodologies.
Mandate specific reasoning algorithms.
Eliminate the need for human engineering oversight.
10. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce lifecycle state models, event-driven cognition, collaborative multi-agent cognition, autonomous learning strategies, and lifecycle optimization mechanisms without changing the canonical definition established by this specification.